.TH labctl 1 "October 2026" "linux-labs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
labctl \- start, grade and reset hands-on Linux labs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B labctl
.I COMMAND
.RI [ LAB ]
.br
.B labctl list
.RB [ \-\-course
.IR COURSE ]
.RB [ \-\-level
.IR LEVEL ]
.br
.B labctl \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B labctl
runs the hands-on Linux labs provided by the linux-labs package.
It prepares a lab, shows its task, grades the final state and resets the
system afterwards.
Labs are installed under
.IR /opt/linux-labs/labs .
Only one lab is active at a time; the active lab is recorded in
.I /opt/linux-labs/.current_lab
and shown as
.B [LAB:<name>]
in the shell prompt.
.PP
Most labs run on a server, normally
.BR servera ,
rather than on the machine where labctl runs.
For such a lab,
.BR start ,
.B grade
and
.B reset
run the lab scripts on the server as root over SSH (as the configured
SSH user, with the configured key), and the task header ends with a line
such as "Work on servera: ssh opsadmin@servera". The student does the work
in that SSH session; the server's prompt shows
.B [LAB:<name>]
too. servera is node 1 of the configuration, serverb node 2, serverc
node 3.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI start " LAB"
Prepare the system for the lab, mark it as the active lab and print its
task. Requires root; the
.B student
user can run it through
.BR sudo .
If the lab setup fails, the lab is not started and labctl exits with the
setup exit status.
While another lab is active,
.B start
refuses with exit status 1 and asks to finish that lab first with
.BR "sudo labctl reset" .
Starting the active lab again is allowed and prepares it from the
beginning.
.TP
.BI task " LAB"
Print the task of the lab again: the header line, the category and level
line, the optional Needs line (what the lab requires: internet, reboot, a
free network interface, a number of nodes), for a lab on a server the
"Work on" line with the ssh command, and the task text. When the lab
has hints, a last line "Stuck? Run labctl hint LAB" follows; the same line
ends the output of
.BR start .
It does not change the system and does not need root.
.TP
.BI grade " LAB"
Check the current state of the system against the lab criteria. Each
criterion is printed on one line with the result PASS or FAIL, followed by
the overall result and the number of criteria met.
.TP
.BI reset " LAB"
Undo the lab setup and the changes the solution makes, and clear the active
lab. Requires root.
.PP
A lab that linux-labs 1.1.0 or older started cannot be graded or
continued, because those versions ran every lab on the workstation:
.BR task ,
.B grade
and
.B start
of that lab print "Error: lab LAB was started with an older version of
linux-labs." and exit 1.
.B reset
of that lab cleans up both on the workstation, where the older version
ran it, and on the lab's server, then clears the active lab. Start the lab
again afterwards.
.TP
.B list [\-\-course \fICOURSE\fP] [\-\-level \fILEVEL\fP]
List the installed labs with their objective.
.B \-\-course
shows only the labs of one course (for example
.BR aoos ) .
.B \-\-level
shows only labs of one level:
.BR beginner ,
.B intermediate
or
.BR advanced .
Both match without regard to case and can be combined.
.TP
.B help
Print the command summary. Running
.B labctl
without arguments does the same.
.TP
.BI hint " LAB"
Show a hint for the lab, one more each time you run it. The first call
prints hint 1, the second prints hints 1 and 2, and so on, with a header
"LAB: hint N of M". Hints go from a direction to a tool to a detail and
never contain the complete solution. After the last hint, the same call
repeats all hints. A lab without hints says so and points to
.BR solution .
The number of hints shown is kept per user in
.IR ~/.local/state/linux-labs/hints/LAB ;
.B start
and
.B reset
delete it. Needs no root.
.TP
.BI hint " LAB " \-\-all
Show every hint of the lab at once. The hint counter does not change.
.TP
.BI solution " LAB " [\-\-yes]
Show the reference solution of the lab. When standard input and standard
output are both terminals, labctl first asks
"This shows the full solution. Continue? [y/N]" and shows the solution only
after y or yes.
.B \-\-yes
skips the question. Without a terminal (a pipe, a script) the solution is
printed without asking. Uses
.B less
when standard output is a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the installed linux-labs version as
.RI "labctl " VERSION
and exit. Also available as
.B \-V
and
.BR version .
Needs no root and changes nothing. A copy run from a git checkout prints
"labctl (development copy)".
.TP
.BI configure " ACTION"
Configure the lab environment (network, nodes for multi-node labs).
.I ACTION
is one of
.BR interactive ,
.BR list ,
.B set
.IR "KEY VALUE" ,
.B validate
or
.BR reset .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo labctl start files-04
labctl task files-04
labctl hint files-04
labctl grade files-04
labctl solution files-04
sudo labctl reset files-04
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B labctl grade
exits 0 when all criteria are met and 1 otherwise.
.B labctl start
and
.B labctl reset
exit 0 on success.
.B labctl start
exits 1 when another lab is active, and
.B labctl task
and
.B labctl grade
exit 1 for a lab started by an older version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to a non-empty value, the grading output is not coloured. Colour
is used only when standard output is a terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/linux-labs/labs/
Installed labs.
.TP
.I /opt/linux-labs/.current_lab
Name of the active lab.
.TP
.I /opt/linux-labs/.current_target
The active lab and the machine its setup ran on, one line
"LAB TARGET". An active lab without this line was started by an older
version of linux-labs.
.TP
.I /var/lib/linux-labs/
On the workstation: labctl's SSH known_hosts for the servers. On a server:
the working copy of the active lab.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/linux-labs/hints/
One file per lab with the number of hints already shown to the user.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/linux-labs/
Student documentation (Markdown).
.TP
.I /etc/linux-labs/config
System-wide configuration; otherwise
.I ~/.config/linux-labs/config
is used.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudo (8)
.SH AUTHORS
linux-labs maintainers.
